<!-- step: reported -->
Every flight that arrives fifteen or more minutes late carries a breakdown of its delay into five reported causes. Over the window, the carriers put {{ v('metric.reported_late_aircraft_share') }} of the cause minutes on a late aircraft, {{ v('metric.reported_carrier_share') }} on the carrier, {{ v('metric.reported_nas_share') }} on the national airspace system, {{ v('metric.reported_weather_share') }} on weather and {{ v('metric.reported_security_share') }} on security. These fields record what was reported, by the carrier, under rules that keep the weather field for extreme weather; ordinary weather that slows the airspace is coded to the national airspace system.

<!-- step: nas -->
The national airspace system's share is highest at {{ v('ch6.nas_top3') }}, airports where capacity, not the weather and not the airline, sets the pace much of the day.

<!-- step: estimated -->
Measured from the weather itself (precipitation, snow, gusts, low cloud, thunder, fog and freezing precipitation at the origin when the flight leaves and at the destination when it lands, on {{ v('ch6.flights_modelled') }} flights between two of the FAA's Core 30 airports, with the inbound aircraft's lateness held constant), weather explains {{ v('ch6.weather_share') }} of arrival delay minutes (interval {{ v('ch6.weather_share.low') }} to {{ v('ch6.weather_share.high') }}). The weather field on the same flights records {{ v('ch6.reported_weather_share') }}, and the national airspace system field {{ v('ch6.reported_nas_share') }}.

